  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14110945/assets/b6abb4332c7601200fec40e8b3f8e6f6f8c81004aaa92af936063625a5c9f2e8.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-3,2).$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -3$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-3$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $y$ for any $y>-3.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-3]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-3]=-3.\n$$",
